WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.690
Watch as we step through
knitting a basic short row sock

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.860
knit from the toe-up.

3
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:10.420
To keep things simple,
I chose to use a solid color.

4
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:12.640
But I'll bet that you can be a whole lot

5
00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:18.340
more creative with your yarn,
color and stitch pattern choices.

6
00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:22.660
The first place to start is to knit
a swatch and establish your gauge.

7
00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:24.420
Without an accurate gauge,

8
00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:28.460
how do you know how many stitches
to cast on and how many rows to knit?

9
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.307
Knit a Swatch.

10
00:00:30.507 --> 00:00:35.500
'Nuff said. One of the reasons to knit a toe-up sock

11
00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.420
is to control of the amount
of yarn you need.

12
00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:43.020
There's nothing worse than running out
of yarn part way through the second sock.

13
00:00:43.050 --> 00:00:46.860
With a little planning
that won't happen to you.

14
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:49.660
Weigh the amount of yarn
you have in grams.

15
00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.620
I'm starting with 168 grams.

16
00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:55.860
We'll come back to this number later.

17
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.300
Next, plan out the size you want to knit.

18
00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:02.420
Measure the foot length
and the foot circumference.

19
00:01:02.450 --> 00:01:05.290
Determine how long you want the leg.

20
00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:07.540
Depending on the amount of yarn you have,

21
00:01:07.570 --> 00:01:11.050
you may need to adjust
the leg length. Again

22
00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.050
this is a reason for knitting toe-up.

23
00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:19.130
Once you know your measurements,
enter them and build your Knit it Now.

24
00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:25.490
Dynamic Pattern. Before doing anything
else, read through the entire pattern.

25
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.660
Identify anything you might not

26
00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:33.060
understand, and look for techniques
that you may need to practice.

27
00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:36.620
In the READ ME section,
we discuss the placement of the toe seam

28
00:01:36.650 --> 00:01:44.000
and knitting a right and left sock so the
seams end up on the inside of the legs.

29
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:48.060
Okay, let's get knitting.

30
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.100
Scrap on the Toe stitches. I used

31
00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:56.020
a green crochet cast-on rag
and knit a row of ravel cord.

32
00:01:56.050 --> 00:01:59.290
This will leave open stitches
that I can graft later

33
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:06.040
to complete my toe.
We'll knit one row and then short row

34
00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.940
shape the toe according
to the pattern instructions.

35
00:02:09.970 --> 00:02:12.360
If you aren't completely confident how

36
00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:18.660
to knit a sock toe or heel, there are
videos available showing some options.

37
00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:22.520
Please take time to watch the videos.

38
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:24.380
Once the toe is complete,

39
00:02:24.410 --> 00:02:28.100
scrap on the remainder of the
stitches needed for the foot.

40
00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:30.890
The "K" dimension in the pattern.

41
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:33.540
I've used a crochet cast-on rag again

42
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:38.890
and one row of yellow ravel cord.
Leaving open stitches that will be grafted

43
00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.760
to the previous cast-on edge
to complete the toe.

44
00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:49.540
With all the foot stitches now on the bed,
knit the foot section.

45
00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:52.900
You may want to add a stitch
pattern or change colors.

46
00:02:52.930 --> 00:02:56.380
Remember, get creative.

47
00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.860
Notice that I added some markers along

48
00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:03.820
this long edge to make
it easier to seam later.

49
00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:10.120
Once the foot is complete,
scrap off the first heel stitches.

50
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:14.200
Short rows the Heel. This is exactly like

51
00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:20.740
the toe short row process
rehang the scrapped off stitches.

52
00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.660
I left my waste yarn in place,

53
00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.740
but you can remove it to make sure
you've picked up all the stitches.

54
00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:28.520
Note the stitch count on your pattern

55
00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:34.180
to double-check.
Knit a few rows according to the pattern.

56
00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:38.700
I like these extra few rows between
the foot and the cuff for the ribbing.

57
00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:42.840
They seem to make a better fitting sock.

58
00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:47.540
Now that the foot is complete,
weigh and record how much yarn you have

59
00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:52.720
left and we'll tell you how
much yarn the foot used.

60
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.700
It's time to knit the cuff,

61
00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:56.580
leg or ribbing.

62
00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:01.220
You have lots of choices
if you want the whole cuff in ribbing,

63
00:04:01.250 --> 00:04:04.140
you can hand-manipulate some ribbing or

64
00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:09.220
hang a ribber cast-on comb
and transfer stitches to the ribber.

65
00:04:09.250 --> 00:04:10.980
I chose to remove the knitting

66
00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:14.780
on a circular hand knitting
needle and hand knit the ribbing.

67
00:04:14.810 --> 00:04:20.380
Before starting to knit my ribbing,
I grafted the toe stitches.

68
00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:26.020
If you aren't fussy, you could just pick
up both sets of stitches and bind off.

69
00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:30.100
Because the seam can be a little bit
bulky, it's best if you plan to have

70
00:04:30.130 --> 00:04:34.060
the toe seam on the top
of the toes for comfort.

71
00:04:34.090 --> 00:04:36.580
I plan to knit my ribbing in the round,

72
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:43.480
so I used mattress stitch to join the side
seams, matching up the yellow markers.

73
00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:48.620
While I was knitting the ribbing, I kept
track of how much yarn I had remaining.

74
00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:51.740
I started with 168 grams.

75
00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:55.720
The foot used 34 grams.

76
00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.340
Now that I'm close to my desired leg
length, I'll weigh the ball again

77
00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:05.340
and confirm that I have
enough yarn remaining.

78
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:09.540
Or maybe I can knit the leg
a little bit longer.

79
00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.140
My leg used 14 grams.

80
00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:17.700
So far I've used a total of 48
grams and have 120 left.

81
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:21.800
No worries about running out.

82
00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:28.260
I'm going to make my leg a little
bit longer and add a rolled edge.

83
00:05:28.290 --> 00:05:32.620
Instead of worrying about a stretchy bind
off in ribbing, I'm going to knit a few

84
00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.140
rows in, stockinette
and then just let it roll.

85
00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:38.280
This is a quick way of adding some style

86
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:42.780
to my sock, and I'll have
a stretchy bind off.

87
00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:45.500
It might seem like socks are complicated,

88
00:05:45.530 --> 00:05:50.460
but with a little planning and taking
it step-by-step, they're not hard.

89
00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.180
I hope you'll give toe-up socks a try.

90
00:05:53.210 --> 00:05:56.000
You'll be glad you did.
Happy sock knitting.

